      "passage": "ST. JUDE HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE\nHEALTH INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (HIT) INTEGRATION DIRECTIVE\nDirective Ref: SJH-IT-2026-042\nTo: Clinical Informatics Teams, Third-Party Digital Health App Developers\nDate: September 2\nSubject: Implementation of HL7® FHIR® R4 Interoperability Endpoints\n\nIn compliance with the 21st Century Cures Act Interoperability and Information Blocking Rule, St. Jude Healthcare Alliance is opening its Electronic Health Record (EHR) systems to standardized Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (FHIR) Release 4 RESTful APIs.\n\nKey Integration Guidelines:\n1. Patient Data Access: Certified third-party healthcare mobile applications may query patient demographics, allergy intolerances, lab results, and medication histories via OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect authentication.\n2. Rate Limiting & Latency Controls: To preserve the stability of production clinical databases during peak hospital hours, API calls are restricted to a maximum burst rate of 100 requests per second per client ID.\n3. Strict Confidentiality: Third-party developers must sign a Business Associate Agreement (BAA) and implement AES-256 encryption for all data at rest and TLS 1.3 for data in transit.\n\nAny developer application exhibiting anomalous query payloads will have its API credentials revoked without notice to safeguard patient data privacy.",
