      "passage": "CIVIL AVIATION & ENVIRONMENTAL REGULATORY COMMISSION\nDIRECTIVE ON SUSTAINABLE AVIATION FUEL (SAF) MANDATE\nDirective Reference: DIR-2026-SAF-09 | Effective Date: January 1\nApplicable To: All Commercial Air Carriers Operating at Domestic Airports\n\n1. Regulatory Mandate Overview\nIn alignment with national net-zero aviation targets, all commercial airlines departing from Tier-1 domestic hubs must incorporate a minimum percentage of certified Sustainable Aviation Fuel (SAF) into their overall jet fuel uplift.\n\n2. Minimum Blending Targets & Timeline:\n• Calendar Year 2026: Minimum 2.0% SAF volume blend across all departing flights.\n• Calendar Year 2028: Minimum 6.0% SAF volume blend.\n• Calendar Year 2030: Minimum 10.0% SAF volume blend.\n\n3. Feedstock Certification & Sustainability Verification:\n• Approved Pathways: Only SAF produced via Hydroprocessed Esters and Fatty Acids (HEFA), Alcohol-to-Jet (ATJ), or Synthetic Power-to-Liquid (PtL) pathways are recognized.\n• Sustainability Criteria: Feedstocks derived from food crops that compete with arable food production (e.g., virgin palm oil or soybean oil) are strictly prohibited. Qualifying SAF must demonstrate a minimum 65% lifecycle greenhouse gas (GHG) reduction compared to conventional fossil jet A-1 fuel, as certified by an approved auditor under the ISCC-EU scheme.\n\n4. Compliance Filings:\nAirlines must submit quarterly fuel uplift and sustainability certificate reconciliations to the Aviation Energy Portal within 30 days of the end of each quarter.",
