      "passage": "HORIZON FINTECH GLOBAL\nDATA PROTECTION & PRIVACY POLICY DIRECTIVE\nSubject: Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA) – Biometric Palm-Vein Authentication\nDate: February 18 | Reference: DPIA-2026-BIO-09\nTarget: Group Compliance Committee, Global IT Security Officers\n\n1. Executive Summary & Regulatory Context\nTo enhance retail branch security and eliminate magnetic stripe card skimming, Horizon Fintech is deploying infrared contactless palm-vein scanning terminals across 250 European branch kiosks. This directive establishes compliance protocols under Article 35 of the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR).\n\n2. Biometric Data Processing & Storage Architecture\n• Zero Raw Biometric Storage: High-resolution infrared vascular pattern images captured by the sensor are immediately processed within a secure hardware cryptographic enclave. Raw vein imagery is permanently discarded within 50 milliseconds of capture.\n• Irreversible Hashing: The unique vascular template is converted into a 512-bit irreversible mathematical hash using SHA-3 hashing with dynamic salting. It is mathematically impossible to reconstruct the physiological palm image from this cryptographic token.\n• Decentralized Storage: Biometric hash tokens are stored in an encrypted vault physically segregated from the primary core customer relational database containing names, national identity numbers, and account balances.\n\n3. User Consent & Alternative Authentication\nUnder GDPR Article 9 (Special Category Data), enrollment in palm-vein recognition is strictly voluntary. Customers who decline biometric enrollment must be provided with equal, frictionless access to all banking services via chip-and-PIN authentication without penalty.",
