      "skill": "Specific Detail Retrieval",
      "passage": "NORDIC MARITIME FLEET MANAGEMENT\nSHIP MODIFICATION & ENVIRONMENTAL COMPLIANCE CIRCULAR\nTo: Chief Engineers, Master Mariners, Fleet Technical Superintendents\nDate: November 3 | Circular ID: NMFM-MARPOL-2026-44\nSubject: Exhaust Gas Cleaning System (EGCS) Closed-Loop Operation in Emission Control Areas (ECAs)\n\nPlease be reminded that in accordance with the International Maritime Organization (IMO) MARPOL Annex VI regulations and regional port authority mandates, all vessels in the Nordic Maritime fleet equipped with hybrid exhaust gas cleaning systems (scrubbers) must strictly observe operating mode transitions.\n\nMandatory Operational Directives:\n1. Mode Transition Threshold:\nWhen navigating within designated Emission Control Areas (Baltic Sea, North Sea, and all European Union territorial waters within 12 nautical miles of the baseline), scrubbers must operate exclusively in Closed-Loop Mode. Open-loop discharge of acidic washwater into coastal waters is strictly prohibited.\n\n2. Caustic Soda (NaOH) Dosing:\nIn closed-loop mode, washwater pH must be maintained between 6.5 and 7.5 using automated 50% sodium hydroxide (NaOH) chemical dosing. Chief engineers must ensure that vessel storage tanks carry a minimum 15-day reserve of chemical reagent prior to departing bunkering ports.\n\n3. Washwater Bleed-Off & Sludge Disposal:\nEffluent bleed-off must pass through the onboard centrifugal water treatment unit. Separated heavy fuel oil sludge and particulate matter must be transferred to the vessel's dedicated holding tank and discharged exclusively to licensed port reception facilities ashore. Under no circumstances may scrubber residue be incinerated onboard.",
